    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nJasmine Liu writes about the clash between the pluralistic make-up of the citizenry of the United States and a K-12 school system that is tasked with teaching nationalism, patriotism, and civic engagement. In particular, public school textbooks portray minority groups and how these primary texts are in conversation with, as Jasmine points out, “scholars who argue citizenship education should be transnationalized and hybridized.”",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Liu, J. (2024). From E Pluribus Unum to E Pluribus Plures: Examining Assimilationist and Nationalist Narratives in American History Textbooks. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/qs587cd8533. https://doi.org/10.25740/qs587cd8533.",
